      "rollout_excess_field_is_not_the_gate_quantity": "the bootstrap's rollout_excess field is the median of the per-window difference (rollout - encoded_target); gate G9 is the difference of the two medians. The bootstrap bounds the sampling of a related quantity, not of the gate value itself, and its report says so in its own field.",
      "script": "scripts/bootstrap_wm_v4_contrasts.py",
      "seed": 20540
    },
    "e1_is_conditioned_on_more_actions_at_the_gate_horizon": "DECLARED BEFORE THE RUN. The evaluator rolls out max(HORIZONS)=16 action steps and reads step 8, for every arm. Under action_chunk=4 the step-8 latent of arm E1 therefore depends on actions 0..10, while E0/E2/E3 depend on actions 0..7. A horizon-8 CEM could not supply actions 8..10; at plan time those slots zero-pad, which is a different computation from the one gated here. So the E1 - E0 contrast is 'joint chunk conditioning AND three extra future actions at the readout horizon', not chunk conditioning alone. The 16-step rollout is required for the cohort-identity assertion to hold, so it is kept and the confound is declared instead of removed. If E1 is the winning arm, the next protocol MUST re-measure it under planner-consistent zero-padding before any of its numbers are carried forward.",
    "g9_reading_condition": {
      "max_m": 0.0389,
      "metric": "decomposition.encoded_target_median_m",
      "rationale": "1.5x v3 arm B's 2.59 cm encoder term. Inside this band G9 is read; outside it the split is not comparable to v3's and G9 is reported as uninterpretable rather than as a pass. A reading rule only: it never changes G9's threshold or its pass/fail computation. It is necessary, NOT sufficient: an encoder degraded to, say, 3.5 cm would sit inside the band and could still post a small excess, which is why no outcome depends on G9."
    },
