  "g9_threshold_derivation": {
    "a_pass_does_not_restore_v2": "at 0.53 cm the excess is still 36 % above v2's carried-over 0.39 cm. Passing G9 halves the v2->v3 regression; it does not undo it.",
    "declared_consequence": "G9 can pass while G1 fails: 0.53 cm on top of arm B's 2.59 cm encoder term is a rollout of about 3.1 cm, still about 2.1x G1's threshold. An arm that passes G9 and fails G1 has not passed the gate set.",
    "not_passable_by_identity_predictor": {
      "note": "persistence minus encoded_target, both far above 0.0053",
      "v3_arm_B_identity_excess_m": 0.015725385397672653,
      "v3_arm_D_identity_excess_m": 0.015076778829097748
    },
    "passable_by_an_undertrained_model_measured_in_the_pilots": {
      "note": "ALL FOUR 120-step pilots passed G9 while failing G2a (0.8493-0.9276, every one worse than v3 arm B's 0.876). Each passed five of the fourteen gates, listed above: G5 is the saturated AUROC TASK-052 flagged as weak, G8a/G8c are floors a non-collapsed latent clears trivially, and G4/G7b on a 120-step model say nothing. This is the measured reason G9 is never read on its own and why the reading condition exists: all five pilots sit far outside the 0.0389 m encoder band.",
